WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.203
Rosa’s 3,600 dollar benefit is fixed.

00:00:02.203 --> 00:00:05.140
If she is disabled for twenty years, inflation

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.710
could cut its buying power in half.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:12.115
A cost-of-living adjustment rider increases the benefit during a claim, usually after

00:00:12.115 --> 00:00:16.521
the first twelve months of disability, by a fixed percentage or by

00:00:16.521 --> 00:00:20.559
the change in the consumer price index, often with a cap.

00:00:20.559 --> 00:00:24.230
It does not raise the benefit before a claim begins.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.535
The rider is one of the more expensive additions

00:00:27.535 --> 00:00:30.839
because long claims are exactly the ones it grows.

00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:34.510
For a 41-year-old with benefits to 65, Theo recommends it.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.696
Rosa expects her income to grow.

00:00:36.696 --> 00:00:41.431
A future increase option, also called a guaranteed insurability rider, lets her buy

00:00:41.431 --> 00:00:45.802
additional monthly benefit at specified option dates or ages without medical underwriting.

00:00:45.802 --> 00:00:48.717
The insurer may still require proof of higher

00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:51.631
income, because benefits must stay within issue limits.

00:00:51.631 --> 00:00:56.002
The rider protects against the day a new diagnosis makes her uninsurable.

00:00:56.002 --> 00:01:00.373
Without it, each increase would require a new application and full underwriting.

00:01:00.373 --> 00:01:03.652
Option dates are usually annual or every few years

00:01:03.652 --> 00:01:06.930
until a stated age, and unused options may expire.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.766
An annual renewable term rider adds temporary monthly benefit at

00:01:10.766 --> 00:01:14.602
a premium that rises each year, like term life insurance.

00:01:14.602 --> 00:01:19.205
It suits a young insured who needs a higher benefit now at

00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:23.809
low initial cost and expects to convert it to level-premium coverage later.

00:01:23.809 --> 00:01:28.028
Two provisions from Lesson 17 reappear in disability policies: relation of

00:01:28.028 --> 00:01:32.248
earnings to insurance, which trims total benefits to actual earnings if

00:01:32.248 --> 00:01:36.468
the insured becomes overinsured, and change of occupation, which adjusts benefits

00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:40.688
when the insured moves to a more or less hazardous job.

00:01:40.688 --> 00:01:44.140
Both keep the benefit tied to real economic loss.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.580
Disability policies often carry lump-sum or expense benefits beside the monthly check.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.020
An accidental death and dismemberment benefit pays a principal sum for accidental

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.460
death and a capital sum for loss of a limb or sight.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.160
A rehabilitation benefit pays for vocational training or retraining that

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.860
helps the insured return to work, in addition to monthly

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.560
benefits, sometimes up to a multiple of the monthly amount.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.630
A medical reimbursement benefit pays medical expenses for an injury that

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.700
does not disable the insured, up to a stated amount, so

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.030
a minor accident produces something even without lost income.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:21.851
Some policies promise money back.

00:02:21.851 --> 00:02:25.856
A return of premium rider refunds a percentage of premiums paid,

00:02:25.856 --> 00:02:29.862
less claims, at set intervals such as every ten years or

00:02:29.862 --> 00:02:33.503
at a stated age, provided the policy stays in force.

00:02:33.503 --> 00:02:37.509
It can raise the premium by half or more, and the

00:02:37.509 --> 00:02:41.150
refund is really the insured’s own money returned without interest.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:44.427
A cash value benefit, found in a few disability

00:02:44.427 --> 00:02:47.705
contracts, accumulates a surrender value like permanent life insurance.

00:02:47.705 --> 00:02:52.074
Both appeal to buyers who dislike paying for something they never use;

00:02:52.074 --> 00:02:56.080
Theo explains that the same dollars invested elsewhere might do better.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.545
Rosa’s finished policy: 3,600 dollars a month, 90-day elimination, benefits to

00:03:01.545 --> 00:03:07.009
65, true own occupation, residual benefit, waiver of premium, 24-hour coverage.

00:03:07.009 --> 00:03:13.467
Riders: cost-of-living adjustment so a long claim keeps pace with prices; future increase

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:19.925
option so she can add benefit as her business grows; a rehabilitation benefit

00:03:19.925 --> 00:03:25.390
so retraining is funded; a modest medical reimbursement for small injuries.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:31.351
She declines return of premium as too expensive for what it returns.

00:03:31.351 --> 00:03:36.319
Each choice answers a specific risk, and each has a

00:03:36.319 --> 00:03:40.790
price the producer should state plainly before she signs.
